<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s1">
  <p class="st-k rv">01 · Challenge</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s1">The data is in the document, the work is in the spreadsheet</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Purchase orders, delivery challans, invoices and bills of lading arrive as PDFs and scans. Teams
    copy the same fields into spreadsheets and ERP systems by hand, which is slow and easy to get
    wrong. Docomate reads those documents with AI, and the product had to make that reliable enough
    to trust.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g4 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">What to extract</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How does a team tell the AI which fields matter in its own documents?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">At what volume</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How do they process one file, a set that belongs together, or hundreds?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Can I trust it</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How do they check what the AI extracted before it reaches other systems?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 04</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Who runs it</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How are companies, users, plans and AI settings managed behind the scenes?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      How might we let a non-technical team teach an AI their documents once, and then trust the data
      it extracts every time after?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s2">
  <p class="st-k rv">02 · Understanding the workflow</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s2">Mapping how a document becomes data</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Before designing screens, I mapped the journey a document takes from upload to export. Each stage
    needed a clear place in the interface, and one of them, validation, needed a person.
  </p>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Six stages the product had to cover</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li><strong>Ingestion</strong>Getting documents in: PDF, JPG, PNG and Word files, one at a time or in bulk.</li>
    <li><strong>Classification</strong>Knowing what kind of document it is and which template applies.</li>
    <li><strong>Extraction</strong>Pulling out the fields that matter, including nested and repeated ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Validation</strong>A person checks and corrects the result: the human in the loop.</li>
    <li><strong>Integration</strong>Exporting to CSV, Word or PDF, or passing data on to other systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Continuous learning</strong>Templates improve as teams correct and reuse them.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Three design principles</h3>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Validation builds trust</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Reviewing results had to be the easiest part of the product, not an afterthought.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Self-serve from day one</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        A team should be able to sign up, build a template and parse documents without help.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Familiar patterns everywhere</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        From parsing to tenants and billing, every screen shares the same structure, so new areas feel
        known.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s3">
  <p class="st-k rv">03 · Defining the experience</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s3">One core loop, three kinds of users</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Everything in the product serves one loop: create a template, parse documents with it, review the
    result and export it. Around that loop sit the people who set it up and keep it running.
  </p>
  <div class="st-stats st-wide rv">
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">4</p>
      <p>steps in the core loop: template, parse, review, export</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">16</p>
      <p>flows designed, from onboarding to pricing configuration</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">3</p>
      <p>user roles, each with its own view of the same product</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Role 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Super admin</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Runs the platform: creates tenants, assigns plans, configures pricing and billing cycles, and
        connects each tenant’s OpenAI key.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Role 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Tenant admin</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Runs a company’s workspace: invites and manages users, keeps the company profile and handles
        the plan and invoices.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Role 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Team member</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Does the daily work: builds templates, uploads documents, reviews extracted data and exports
        it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s4">
  <p class="st-k rv">04 · Key UX decisions</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s4">Four decisions that shape the product</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Each decision answers one of the questions teams arrive with. Follow the core loop first, then see
    each decision beside the screen it shaped.
  </p>

  <h3 class="st-sub rv">The core loop: from a new document type to exported data</h3>
  <div class="st-wide st-tabset--steady rv">
    <div class="st-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Core loop steps">
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-j1" aria-controls="p-j1">1. Create a template</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-j2" aria-controls="p-j2">2. Upload documents</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-j3" aria-controls="p-j3">3. Review the result</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-j4" aria-controls="p-j4">4. Export</button>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-j1" aria-labelledby="t-j1">
      <p class="cs__p">
        Upload one sample document. The AI proposes its key information in groups, and the team keeps,
        renames or switches off each field.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-j2" aria-labelledby="t-j2">
      <p class="cs__p">
        Pick the template, choose Single, Merge or Bulk, fill in any meta data and drop in the files.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-j3" aria-labelledby="t-j3">
      <p class="cs__p">
        The source document sits on the left and the extracted table on the right, so every value can
        be checked against where it came from.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-j4" aria-labelledby="t-j4">
      <p class="cs__p">Export as CSV, PDF or Word, straight from the review screen.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision A</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Teach the AI once, with a template</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Instead of setting up fields from a blank form, the team starts from a real document. The AI
          suggests its key information as nested groups (company, address, plant, contacts), and each
          group has a toggle to keep or drop it and a field to rename it to the team’s own terms.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/docomate-template.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="910"
        alt="Template preview: a purchase order on the left and, on the right, Extracted Key Information in nested groups such as Company Details, Company Address and Plant Details. Each group has a toggle, and each field shows an Enter new name input beside the extracted value and a checkbox."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Creating a template from a sample purchase order</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision B</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Keep the source beside the data</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Validation is where trust is won. Results open next to the original document, with zoom on
          the source and an editable table on the right. Teams can add a missing row, reset their
          changes or expand the table to full width when a document has many columns.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/docomate-review.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="934"
        alt="Parsed Documents page for the template ZARA - Set: the source purchase order with zoom controls on the left, and a Key Information Preview table on the right with style references, style descriptions, PO numbers, delivery countries and colours. Add New Row, Reset and Export as buttons sit below."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Review: every extracted value next to the page it came from</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision C</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">One upload screen for one file or hundreds</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Single, Merge and Bulk sit side by side as parsing types, so the same screen handles one
          invoice, a set of pages that belong together, or a batch. The most used templates are listed
          beside the form, and meta data fields appear only when the chosen template needs them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/docomate-upload.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="1039"
        alt="Document Parsing page: a Template dropdown set to ZARA - Set, Parsing Type options Single, Merge and Bulk with Merge selected, six meta data fields, a drag and drop upload area and four uploaded files with progress bars. A Most Used Templates list sits on the right."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Parsing type, meta data and upload on one screen</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision D</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Show limits before they get in the way</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Each plan has a document limit, and new teams start on a free trial. Usage is always visible: a
          donut chart on the dashboard, a banner counting down the trial, and an inline warning on the
          upload screen when only a few parses remain, each with a direct Upgrade Now link.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/docomate-limit.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="1103"
        alt="The Document Parsing page with files uploaded and, above the Cancel and Submit buttons, a red notice reading Only 2 parsing remaining in your free trial period, followed by an Upgrade Now link."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>A warning in context, before the trial runs out</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

